{
  "quest.0F6661FFB09F81B8.quest_desc": [
    "You'll soon need a Metal Arrow Head to make a certain tool. The problem is that you have to make a Mold first in order to make the Arrow Head. The fastest way is to craft Flint Arrow Heads in a Crafting Table from a piece of Stone and some Flint (yields 4 Flint Arrow Heads), or with a File and Flint (yields 6 Flint Arrow Heads). Then you can make a Clay Mold, combine one of the Arrow Heads with the Mold to make a Clay Arrow Mold, and put it in a Furnace to fire it.",
    "",
    "So what are Flint Arrow Heads good for? You can combine a Headless Wood Arrow with a Flint Arrow Head to make a Flint Arrow. Headless Wood Arrows can be obtained as drops from killing Skeletons, or made by combining a Stick and a Feather in a Crafting Table.",
    "",
    "Warning: don't shoot Creepers with Flint Arrows. Once a Creeper is set on fire its fuse is lit too, and then the Creeper will explode. The same thing happens if you light a Creeper with Flint and Steel (Flint and Iron). So unless you plan on blast mining, I don't recommend shooting Creepers with Flint Arrows.",
    "",
    "Note: the Flint Knife, Flint Pickaxe and Flint Axe won't light a Creeper's fuse, but they will set the Creeper on fire.",
  ],
  "quest.22B86EDBC7256793.quest_desc": [
    "Either way, you now have an Anvil that is 8 times better than a Stone Anvil.",
  ],
  "quest.1F5E756BA7897CA9.quest_desc": [
    "Used to manually perform some Mixer recipes.",
    "",
    "Right-click a side with a fluid container to insert/extract fluids.",
    "",
    "There are regular and table-top versions.",
    "",
    "This is used here to make Bronze Compound. Unlike gt5, this method has no loss.",
  ],
  "quest.1241866523ED736C.quest_desc": [
    "Because Brick Burning Boxes are inefficient and low-powered, it's recommended to make two stacks each of Brick Burning Boxes and Crucibles.",
  ],
  "quest.60B0D16EEAFB3DCB.title": "Say Goodbye to the Alloy Furnace! / Bronze Alloy Making",
  "quest.71E739D41A905410.quest_desc": [
    "The Anvil is an important processing block.",
    "",
    "The Anvil has three interactable faces: the top, the large side, and the small side.",
    "",
    "The top face has two item slots; right-click to place/take out items.",
    "When there is no item on the top face, right-clicking with a Hammer places the Hammer on it.",
    "When there is an item on it, right-clicking with a Hammer performs regular Anvil recipes.",
    "",
    "Right-clicking the large side and the small side with a Hammer performs the Anvil (Big) and Anvil (Small) recipes respectively.",
    "When you look at them, Jade will show a hint.",
    "",
    "Anvils have durability and break when it runs out.",
    "However, used metal Anvils can be recycled in a Crucible without loss.",
    "Among Stone Anvils, Blackstone and Red/Black Granite Anvils are the best.",
    "In the early game, a Lead Anvil is recommended.",
  ],
  "quest.2223FC02136C43ED.quest_desc": [
    "You can choose to grind out a Bronze Burning Box and Bronze tools to get your Crucible going.",
    "",
    "Or you can use the extremely inefficient Brick Burning Box to smelt one crucible of Bronze, then fill in the Bronze Burning Box and Bronze tools afterwards.",
    "",
    "Since the Crucible + Mold combo can process most materials without loss, the latter wastes less material.",
    "",
    "The questline splits into two branches at this stage; you only need to pick one.",
  ],
  "quest.17616608AED198A8.quest_desc": [
    "A multiblock machine that turns Logs into Charcoal, Coal into Coal Coke, and Lignite Coal into Lignite Coke to raise their fuel value, with Creosote Oil as a byproduct.",
    "",
    "In particular, Willow Logs yield double the Charcoal and Creosote Oil, and Willow grows fast too. If you can't find Coal for Steelmaking, look for Willow saplings in Swamp biomes.",
  ],
  "quest.17F7F72BB3669B9A.quest_desc": [
    "Do you need something to transport Water?",
    "",
    "Wooden Barrels are cheap, and they not only let you get started with farming, but also let you fill a Cauldron (if you found one) to wash ores.",
    "",
    "In the Steam Age you'll also need it to make Treated Wood, which is used to make Fluid Pipes. By then you should already have some Copper available. You can also use Lead, Tin or Zinc.",
    "",
    "If for some reason you've already made some Bronze or Brass, you can also use either of those materials to make a Barrel.",
  ],
  "quest.6593AA566467EE37.quest_desc": [
    "We need a Quicksilver Thermometer to know the Crucible's temperature accurately. To make a Quicksilver Thermometer you need Mercury. So how do you get Mercury?",
    "",
    "We get Mercury from Cinnabar Ore. Cinnabar can be found in Redstone Ore Veins, but there are more Redstone Veins without Cinnabar than with it. You need a Tier 2 Pickaxe to mine the real Cinnabar Ore that generates in Komatiite. Near the top of the vein there should be Small Cinnabar Ore. Those are the ones you want. If you have no luck finding a Cinnabar vein in the Overworld, you can also find Small Cinnabar Ore near the top of the world in the Nether. Another option is washing Crushed Redstone Ore, but that may take more than a stack of Crushed Redstone, since you only have a chance of getting one Tiny Purified Cinnabar Ore. You'll also need a Cauldron for ore washing. You can find a Cauldron in the world, or make one from Iron Plates. You can also wash Crushed Stibnite Ore for a chance at one Tiny Purified Cinnabar Ore.",
    "",
    "If you find a vanilla Ore Vein containing Redstone, Gold and/or Diamond, you can use a Mortar to knock Cinnabar Dust out of the Redstone Ore blocks. This may actually be the best way to get enough Cinnabar early on to make a Quicksilver Thermometer.",
    "",
    "You need to collect enough to make 3 Crushed/Purified Cinnabar Ores or 3 portions of Cinnabar Dust. You might want to gather a little extra in case something goes wrong. Each Cinnabar makes 3 Mercury Nuggets, and it takes 9 nuggets' worth to fill a Glass Bottle. While collecting Cinnabar you should also collect some Small Redstone Ore, since you'll need it soon.",
    "{@pagebreak}",
    "Once you've collected 3 Cinnabar Ores/Cinnabar Dust, you need to extract the Mercury from the Cinnabar. Mercury is an interesting metal — it's liquid at room temperature. So GregTech 6 gives you the option of using a Crucible. Throw 3 Crushed/Purified Cinnabar Ores or 3 portions of Cinnabar Dust into the Crucible. Warning: if you're using Crushed Cinnabar, use a Shovel to remove the Scrap first, then continue. Next, light the Burning Box with 1 or 2 Sticks. That should give you Mercury.",
    "",
    "The last step is to right-click the upper edge of one side of the Crucible with a Glass Bottle to collect the Mercury into the Glass Bottle. Be careful not to keep right-clicking, because you might drink the Mercury. If you drink Mercury you'll very likely die of Mercury Poisoning (and you'd better not, even if you're really thirsty!).",
    "",
    "Note: if you happen to obtain a Cinnabar Gem, you can put the gem on an Anvil and hammer it into a Chipped Cinnabar. Finally, hammer the Chipped Cinnabar to get a Tiny Pile of Cinnabar Dust. You can also do the same with Crushed Cinnabar to get 3 portions of Cinnabar Dust. That will complete the quest.",
  ],
  "quest.0A83ECE13D715296.quest_desc": [
    "A device that provides HU must be placed below the Crucible.",
    "",
    "For every 100kg of items in the Crucible (see the corresponding item's tooltip for details, including the Crucible's own mass), 1 HU is needed to raise the temperature by 1K.",
    "Because Ceramic Crucibles have extremely low heat mass, never heat one while it's empty.",
    "",
    "The Crucible's initial temperature depends on altitude and biome.",
    "",
    "When the Crucible's temperature exceeds its limit it explodes, its contents vanish, and it leaves behind Fire. It destroys less-hard blocks and damages entities, and usually blows the player who is casting next to it sky-high.",
    "When the items inside the Crucible reach their boiling point, they vaporize and damage nearby players.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 1: There are 16 units of visible item slots inside, which heat up together when the Crucible is heated.",
    "There is a hidden inventory of 64 units; when more items are inserted than the visible slots can hold, they are automatically put there, and they are automatically imported once the Crucible has free space.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 2: With no HU input, it cools down naturally at 2K/s until it reaches ambient temperature.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 3: Above 394K, contact causes damage; this can be negated by the Fire Resistance buff or a Hazmat Suit.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 4: Items put into the Crucible become \"Scrap\", equivalent to 1/9 of an Ingot; remove Scrap by right-clicking the top with an empty hand or a Shovel. If you remove Scrap with an empty hand, you take damage according to Mechanic 3.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 5: The Crucible can store 16000mB of non-liquefied metal fluid; if Lava is inserted while the temperature is below 1300K, it automatically solidifies into Obsidian and raises the Crucible's temperature (Water can lower the temperature, but you will be scalded by the vaporization effect).",
    "",
    "Mechanic 6: The Crucible can melt ores and metals; after melting, right-click the fluid inside the Crucible with a Measuring Pot/Tank to scoop it out.",
    "PS: This process cannot be automated; this is intentional. For automated melting, use a Smelter/Melter instead.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 7: When exposed to rain, Water appears inside the Crucible; when there is Water in the Crucible, heating it to 373K (100°C) vaporizes the Water and damages nearby players, so remember to shelter your Crucible from rain.",
    "",
    "Mechanic 8: When the difference between the Crucible's temperature and its meltdown temperature is within 100K, the Crucible turns yellow.",
    "PS: Since Crucibles can be dyed, you can dye one yellow, or stick Gold Plates on the sides and bottom of a pipe, to disguise it as a Crucible that is about to explode and scare your friends.",
  ],
  "quest.23120973710EA78A.title": "Tin Alloy Plate",
  "quest.0A83ECE13D715296.title": "Crucible Usage Tutorial",
  "quest.30CF96F9624A4C63.title": "Ore Types",
  "quest.30CF96F9624A4C63.quest_desc": [
    "Ores come in two kinds: Small Ores and regular Ores.",
    "",
    "Small Ores drop Crushed Ores, while regular Ores drop Raw Ores.",
    "",
    "Small Ores have a Mining Level one tier lower than regular Ores, so you can mine Small Native Iridium Ore with a level 3 Pickaxe.",
  ],
  "quest.3F38718E4213C36F.quest_desc": [
    "Crafting a Furnace consumes an ignition tool, and Flint and Tinder is the obvious choice.",
    "",
    "One Furnace is definitely not enough, so make several.",
  ],
  "quest.2B41D523E84B43D1.quest_desc": [
    "Unlike GregTech 5, the GregTech 6 Axe does not include tree-felling, but it can still strip the bark off Logs, and stripped Logs can be split into more Planks.",
    "",
    "Besides fighting mobs, the Knife can also cut Grass (cutting Grass doesn't consume durability). It's recommended to make one of Flint; Flint weapons come with Fire Aspect, saving you the step of cooking food.",
    "",
    "The Pickaxe's purpose needs no explanation; making one of Red/Black Granite is strongly recommended.",
    "",
    "The Hammer can smash Stone into Cobblestone and Cobblestone into the corresponding Rocks (do this if you're short on Rocks), and can also perform Anvil recipes.",
    "The Hammer can also directly crush non-Small Ores into Crushed Ores; do this when you run into a Coal ore layer.",
  ],
  "quest.60B0D16EEAFB3DCB.quest_desc": [
    "If you want to make Bronze in bulk, you must use the Crucible to cast alloys! Turn the page for more!",
    "",
    "Take Bronze as an example: to make Bronze you need to add 1 unit of Tin (a Plate or anything equivalent to 1 Ingot) to 3 units of Copper. That is, the ratio of Copper to Tin is 3:1. The best approach is to first make a pile of Copper Ingots from your Crushed Copper Ore or Tetrahedrite, and some Tin Ingots from Crushed Tin Ore or Cassiterite. Once you have clean, good Copper Ingots and Tin Ingots, clean any leftover Scrap out of the Crucible and then you can make some Bronze.",
    "",
    "Note: you can right-click the Crucible with an empty hand (if the Crucible is cold) or with a Shovel (a Universal Spade works too!) to clean it.",
    "",
    "Warning: only use your hand while the Crucible is cold; otherwise you may get hurt or die by accident.",
    "",
    "You'll see the Tin melt first, and then once the Copper melts they'll mix to form Bronze.",
    "",
    "Recipe:",
    "3 Copper Ingot/Dust + 1 Tin Ingot/Dust => [Crucible 1357°K] => 4 Bronze Ingot/Plate",
    "{@pagebreak}",
    "Pro Tip 1: when using Crushed Ore in the Crucible it produces stony Scrap that you need to remove. If you use a Shovel, you can clear all the stony Scrap in one go. Just make sure you do it before you light the Burning Box and before the ore starts melting.",
    "",
    "Pro Tip 2: take the Copper and Tin out as Ingots/Plates so you get exact amounts. If you look at Crushed or Purified Copper or Tin, you'll notice you get more than 1 unit of Copper or Tin. So when you make Bronze, you need the Copper to Tin ratio to be 3:1.",
    "",
    "Search the Crucible alloy recipes for the alloy you want to make! Besides Bronze, there are many more alloys!",
  ],
  "quest.010BBDF1B38D93B0.title": "Preparing for the Next Stage",
  "quest.153BDFDA9F71455F.quest_desc": [
    "Crafting a Bronze Burning Box requires a Wrench.",
    "A Bronze Wrench is recommended.",
    "",
    "If you're short on Bronze, you can also craft it with a Lead Wrench.",
    "But the Lead Wrench's Mining Level is too low — once placed, it can't be removed, so think twice.",
  ],
  "quest.63A76D55BA30DB35.quest_desc": [
    "The Resin/Sap Bag is a machine used to extract Sticky Resin/Maple Syrup/Rainbow Sap from GT6 Rubber/Maple/Rainbowood trees.",
    "",
    "Place it on the resin hole of a GT6 Rubber Tree/Maple/Rainbowood tree (Rubber Trees generate one naturally; for Maple and Rainbowood trees you need to drill one with a Hand Drill) and it will collect the resin automatically.",
    "",
    "When collection is done, right-click to take out the Sticky Resin, or right-click with a fluid container to collect the Maple Syrup/Rainbow Sap.",
    "",
    "This is the only way to obtain Sticky Resin.",
    "",
    "It's rather slow, so it's recommended to make one early.",
  ],
  "quest.1E8B2A8FC92B7ED2.quest_subtitle": "This is not TerraFirmaCraft",
  "quest.05A6926BD20D0CEC.quest_desc": [
    "GregTech 6 does not change the Crafting Table recipes.",
    "",
    "However, you'll find that one Log only crafts into 2 Planks.",
  ],
  "quest.24CE9ADFA2E0BB49.title": "How to Use Molds",
  "quest.5BF75BDCC125400A.title": "Choosing a Base Location",
  "quest.657750F062A5A84F.title": "How Do I Shut the Fire Off?",
  "quest.2B41D523E84B43D1.title": "Early Game Tools",
  "quest.52C1F342E19D3574.title": "About Indicators",
  "quest.62DF727AFE6590B2.quest_desc": [
    "Metal tools are far better than the ones made from Rocks.",
    "",
    "Your tools can be made from almost any metal or alloy. At this stage of GT6, your best materials are Lead and Bronze.",
    "",
    "You can use JEI to check the durability of each metal and alloy; just hover over the pure material item and its tool properties will be shown. Some materials are better than others. For example, Copper is a terrible tool material because the tool breaks after one or two uses.",
    "",
    "{@pagebreak}",
    "Pro Tip: you can make Tool Molds for certain tools, such as the Hammer, File, Pickaxe, Axe and so on. Unfortunately, for more complex tools such as the Wrench, Monkey Wrench, Wire Cutter or Pincers, you can't make these with Molds. For simple tools (Hammer, File, etc.), the key is to make the complete tool with its Stick first, before combining the tool with the Clay Mold in a Crafting Table. If you combine the Mold and the Tool Head, you'll lose the Tool Head; but if you combine the complete tool, it only consumes a little tool durability. Once you have these Tool Molds, you can cast Tool Heads directly from the Crucible. Tool Molds produce Raw Tool Heads. You need to use a File to work a Raw Tool Head into a Tool Head, or a Grindstone once you've made Iron.",
  ],
  "quest.0BD6D2C5804C0381.quest_desc": [
    "Every material has three basic properties:",
    "Melting Point (M), Boiling Point (B), and Mass (W).",
    "These three basic properties require the advanced tooltip to be enabled.",
    "",
    "Materials used for tools also have three additional tool properties, plus optional built-in enchantments:",
    "Quality (Q), Speed Factor (S), and Durability Factor (D).",
    "Quality determines the tool's Mining Level.",
    "Speed Factor determines the tool's mining speed.",
    "Durability Factor determines the tool's durability.",
    "Built-in enchantments are the enchantments the tool has after being crafted.",
    "By the way, some tool types also have built-in enchantments.",
  ],
  "quest.6B8F9A91CFD3641F.quest_desc": [
    "A very practical crafting table.",
    "",
    "The two sides with a drawer pattern can be opened to access a 36-slot buffer, and the materials inside are automatically replenished when crafting.",
    "",
    "It can pull in a chain from nearby \"Tanks\" and \"Drawers\", but they must be GT6's own containers.",
    "",
    "Tanks will automatically output fluid into the crafting table (if needed).",
  ],
  "quest.5BF75BDCC125400A.quest_desc": [
    "It's recommended to build your base at the border between a Desert and a River.",
    "",
    "Desert surfaces generate many sand Small Ores of Lead, Copper, and Tin.",
    "These Small Ores can be mined by hand or with a Shovel, and their quantity is enough to support early progression.",
    "Also, it doesn't rain in Deserts, saving you from building rain shelters for your machines.",
    "",
    "GregTech 6 removed infinite Water; the best source of infinite Water is a Drain placed in a River biome.",
  ],
  "quest.657750F062A5A84F.quest_desc": [
    "GTQuality provides a way to shut the fire off with a Trapdoor!",
    "",
    "Now, if a Trapdoor is in the upright position it acts like a solid block and doesn't supply Air to the Burning Box, and if it's in the lowered position it supplies Air to the Burning Box!",
    "",
    "Throw away those useless Pistons (but really, don't throw them in the Trash Can!)",
  ],
  "quest.354DBEA61B0C421A.quest_desc": [
    "Craft the Grass cut with a Knife into Grass Bales; placed Grass Bales will dry out or rot, which you can tell from the info on the item tooltip.",
    "",
    "Besides being a fast-burning fuel, Dry Grass can also be used to make Flint and Tinder.",
    "Flint and Tinder is the easiest fire-starting material to obtain, usable for lighting fires or crafting a Furnace.",
  ],
  "quest.44EB8782F2412B47.quest_desc": [
    "Now it's time to make some extra tools; these will carry you through the Stone Age and beyond.",
    "",
    "&nSaw&r, for cutting Sticks and Wood",
    "&nScrewdriver&r, for assembling anything that contains Screws",
    "&nPincers&r, for pulling items out of Molds quickly without burning yourself to death. Pincers let you grab hot items out of a Mold, so you can keep using the Mold &lsooner&r, before the molten metal in the Crucible solidifies.",
    "The &nSoft Hammer&r is useful for making wooden items (like your first Pipes), and can also reset machines and sensors to their default settings",
    "Finally, a &nWire Cutter&r will help you make some Wires for machines, and handle the Red Alloy Wires needed for automation",
    "",
    "Warning: you need to make a Hand Drill to make the Soft Hammer Head out of Wood. The early-game Hand Drill is made from 3 Sticks (any wood), one Metal Arrow Head and 2 Metal Bolts. The Arrow Head and Bolts must be made of the same material. You can make them by casting metal into the Arrow Head Mold and Bolt Mold in a Crucible. Bronze works, and so does Lead.",
    "{@pagebreak}",
    "Pro Tip: you can make the Molds for the Saw and Screwdriver using the finished tool and a Clay Mold. If you use the complete tool, the tool takes some durability damage but isn't consumed. If you use only the Tool Head, it's consumed when making the Mold. Pincers, Soft Hammer and Wire Cutter don't have Molds. You can combine the Screwdriver, Saw and Wire Cutter into a Multitool.",
    "",
    "New use: Greg added the ability to right-click a Chest with Pincers. Doing so pulls the contents of the Chest into your inventory. It prioritizes stackable items. This should help a lot with sorting through the contents of opened loot chests at your base",
  ],
  "quest.17F7F72BB3669B9A.title": "Storing Water Without an Iron Bucket",
  "quest.60B0D16EEAFB3DCB.quest_subtitle": "And Say Goodbye to the Industrial Blast Furnace!",
  "quest.77FB75598D7D9A96.quest_desc": [
    "Burning Boxes come in Solid, Liquid, Gas, and Fluidized Bed varieties:",
    "",
    "Solid: right-click the front of the Burning Box with fuel in hand to insert it, or input it with Hoppers and Item Pipes. While working it produces Ashes; scrape them out by right-clicking the front with an empty hand or a Shovel, or output them automatically with a Hopper. Scraping with your hand while it's working causes damage; using a Shovel won't burn you, but consumes durability;",
    "",
    "Liquid/Gas: pipe the fluid into the Burning Box; while working it produces fluid (which is automatically vented into the air; use a Nozzle to collect and output it).",
    "",
    "Fluidized Bed: consumes a small amount of Molten Calcite while working. Accepted fuels include: Coal, Coal Coke, Anthracite Coal, Lignite Coal, Lignite Coke, Charcoal, Peat, Bituminous Peat, Petroleum Coke, Prismane, and Lonsdaleite in dusts of all sizes.",
    "Each unit of fuel consumed also consumes 0.5 units of Molten Calcite.",
    "For the same fuel, grinding it into Dust and feeding it into a Fluidized Bed Burning Box yields 3 times the HU.",
    "A Fluidized Bed Burning Box has 4 times the power of other Burning Box types of the same material.",
    "",
    "Burning Boxes have corresponding Dense versions with 4 times the power of the non-Dense version.",
    "",
    "Blocking the front of a Burning Box with a solid block won't extinguish it immediately; it waits until the current fuel finishes burning. Liquid and Gas fuels burn for only a few ticks, so they stop almost instantly when blocked.",
    "A Solid Burning Box's shut-off time depends on the fuel's heat value, so when using a Solid Burning Box to heat a Crucible, try to use fuel with a low heat value per unit (such as Tiny Crushed Coal Ore), to avoid the tragedy of being unable to shut the fire off and blowing up your Crucible.",
  ],
  "quest.52C1F342E19D3574.quest_subtitle": "This is very TerraFirmaCraft",
  "quest.4D7BC873DE055F51.quest_desc": [
    "Let's make a Thermometer Sensor so you can read the temperature directly, instead of right-clicking with a Quicksilver Thermometer and checking the chat to find out how hot the Crucible is! This sensor can also be switched to other modes (read the tooltip for how to switch), triggering a Redstone signal when certain conditions are met. You'll see more about this in the \"Early Automation\" quest.",
    "",
    "You need to make some Red Alloy Plates, and use your Anvil and Hammer to make Red Alloy Foil. Next, you can use a Wire Cutter to make the Fine Red Alloy Wire needed for the Thermometer Sensor. You also need some Redstone. You can use regular Redstone Gems or Redstone Dust.",
    "",
    "Just make one Double Tin Alloy Plate and add your Quicksilver Thermometer to craft a Thermometer Sensor.",
    "",
    "To use the sensor, just place it on the side of the Crucible and that's it. Now you can see the Crucible's temperature.",
  ],
  "quest.5478330D8B7A7CD6.quest_desc": [
    "By now you probably have chest after chest full of Cobblestone, Black Ashes, Dirt, Gravel and Sand. You need a different storage method so you can find what you need. So let's use GregTech Storage Barrels.",
    "",
    "These Barrels are made from Wood and a few Long Rods. The best metals at this point are Lead, Bronze or Brass. Long Rods can be made on an Anvil from 2 regular Rods, or with a Long Rod Mold.",
    "",
    "Each Wooden Item Barrel can store 5,000 of the same item. This makes these barrels perfect for storing your Cobblestone, Clay or other bulk items.",
    "",
    "But that's not all the Barrel can do. The GregTech 6 Item Barrel comes with a free Compacting Drawer feature! You can configure one form (usually the form of the item inserted first), and then put in materials of various other forms, such as Dust, Tiny Pile of Dust, 1/72nd of a Pile of Dust, Block of Dust (9 Dusts), etc.; they'll all be converted automatically to the configured form without voiding anything! (If there isn't enough to convert, they go into the internal storage.)",
  ],
  "quest.6C03DD5F6BECB49B.quest_desc": [
    "Now that you've set up your Crucible and have tools, you still need something to make Double Plates, Curved Plates and Springs. At this early stage of the tech line you need an Anvil. Since we already have Bronze, a Bronze Anvil will last much longer than those Stone Anvils.",
    "",
    "If unfortunately you're really short on Bronze, you can make a Lead Anvil, a Red Granite Anvil or a Black Granite Anvil first, until you find more Copper and Tin.",
  ],
  "quest.1C3A64A7E29ED1B4.title": "Crucible",
  "quest.7DC186A95EE05916.quest_desc": [
    "Bronze Compound can be smelted into Bronze Nuggets.",
    "",
    "The amount the quest asks for is nowhere near enough; considering the losses on this route, half a stack of Bronze Ingots is recommended.",
  ],
  "quest.2223FC02136C43ED.title": "Choosing an Early Route",
  "quest.71E739D41A905410.title": "How to Use the Anvil",
  "quest.62DF727AFE6590B2.title": "Time to Make Some Metal Tools",
  "quest.3C0D4962070EB7CC.title": "Tool Changes",
  "quest.23120973710EA78A.quest_desc": [
    "Tin Alloy Plates, not Tin Plates!",
    "",
    "This is a very practical alloy that lets you make all sorts of Sensors in block form",
    "",
    "If you don't know how to make alloys yet, see \"Say Goodbye to the Alloy Furnace! / Alloy Making\"",
  ],
  "quest.010BBDF1B38D93B0.quest_desc": [
    "Congratulations — only one last step stands between you and the Steam Age.",
    "",
    "But before that, gather enough materials.",
    "",
    "Since gt6's progression is not linear, the Oil Age, Chemical Engineering, and Electric Age chapters after the Steam Age are non-linear and must be done interleaved.",
    "You'll be jumping back and forth between chapters.",
    "",
    "&bChapter 2: Roaring Steam is unlocked!&r",
    "May your Boiler never explode, Employee.",
  ],
  "quest.1C3A64A7E29ED1B4.quest_desc": [
    "The Crucible is an important processing tool.",
    "In the early game, only Ceramic Crucibles are recommended.",
    "",
    "The Crucible Crossing can expand the Mold's attachment points.",
    "",
    "The Mold is responsible for solidifying molten materials.",
    "Molds need to be carved with a Chisel before use, with the exception of Ceramic Molds.",
    "Ceramic Molds can be shaped before firing; once shaped and fired, no carving is needed.",
    "",
    "&bChapter: Crucible Tech is unlocked!&r",
  ],
  "quest.3EC644F22486D816.quest_desc": [
    "A Red Alloy Plate cast from Copper and Redstone",
    "",
    "This is a very practical alloy",
    "",
    "If you make it into Wire, it's also the material for the Redstone cables you can string up in the air early on!",
    "",
    "If you don't know how to make alloys yet, see \"Say Goodbye to the Alloy Furnace! / Alloy Making\"",
  ],
  "quest.1E8B2A8FC92B7ED2.title": "Rocks and Sticks",
  "quest.0BD6D2C5804C0381.title": "Material Properties",
  "quest.5D985B228604E8F6.quest_desc": [
    "Important crafting materials.",
    "Plates are crafted and processed with an Anvil, while Rods are crafted with a File, though both involve losses.",
    "Once you have a Crucible, they can be processed without loss.",
  ],
  "quest.4066668448C48BDF.title": "Iron Smelting",
  "quest.77FB75598D7D9A96.title": "About Burning Boxes",
  "quest.071733F0C24A8370.quest_desc": [
    "A Bronze Burning Box's 24HU of power is no longer enough for a Crucible.",
    "Upgrade your Burning Box!",
    "A Fluidized Bed Burning Box (Bronze) is a good choice, but it consumes Molten Calcite.",
    "",
    "If you've mined Chromite, you can also make a Chromium Burning Box, which already has 112HU of power.",
    "However, a Chromium Burning Box requires a Tier 3 Wrench, so it's recommended to make a Chromium Wrench too.",
  ],
  "quest.4D7BC873DE055F51.title": "Manual Ignition or Manual Shutoff?",
  "quest.0EBB329F010FC2D3.quest_desc": [
    "The Bathing Pot can be used in place of some of the Bath's recipes, so why not make one?",
    "",
    "You'll need it when processing Treated Wood",
    "",
    "&4It can't run recipes that require high-temperature fluids, such as galvanizing Steel; you need a Stainless Steel Bathing Pot for that&r",
  ],
  "quest.071733F0C24A8370.title": "Burning Box Upgrade",
  "quest.24CE9ADFA2E0BB49.quest_desc": [
    "Right-click a Mold with a Chisel to carve it.",
    "",
    "You can draw the fluid out of the Crucible through its side by connecting a Crucible Crossing to the side of the Crucible, and cast that way.",
    "Alternatively, it can receive fluid from the auto-output direction of Pipes and Tanks to cast as well (feeding in horizontally through a Pipe usually doesn't drain completely; feeding from above is better).",
    "",
    "After the fluid cools and solidifies in the Mold, right-click to take it out; but if it's still too hot you'll still be scalded (using Pincers or wearing a full Heat Protection Suit avoids this).",
    "",
    "Right-click the center of the top face with a Monkey Wrench to toggle auto-input (the default is auto-input when there is no redstone signal).",
    "Right-click the edge of the top face with a Monkey Wrench to enable/disable auto-input on that side (no auto-input by default).",
    "",
    "It has the same maximum temperature as a Crucible of the same material; if it receives fluid that is too hot, it turns into a flowing Lava block.",
    "Unlike the Crucible, when this block breaks it doesn't directly damage its surroundings.",
    "",
    "There's more than one way to carve it; the Mold is recognized as correct as long as the relative positions of the indentations are right (mirror symmetry is also fine).",
  ],
  "quest.4066668448C48BDF.quest_desc": [
    "Iron ores include Black Sand, Hematite, and Limonite.",
    "Mining the latter two iron ores is not recommended.",
    "It's recommended to look for Black Sand in River biomes.",
    "Black Sand is the best iron ore and also the source of Vanadium Pentoxide; note that one Black Sand equals 9 parts Magnetite.",
    "",
    "The carbon used for Iron smelting can be Pure Carbon, Coal Coke (decomposes into an equal amount of Carbon at high temperature), Charcoal (decomposes into half the amount of Carbon at high temperature), or Coal (decomposes into half the amount of Carbon at high temperature); Lignite Coal, Lignite Coke, and Petroleum Coke cannot be used.",
    "In particular, RC Coke can't go into the Crucible, but RC Coke Blocks can.",
    "",
    "Since the Crucible only has 16 units of heating slots, it's recommended to put in the carbon first and then the iron ore; Charcoal or Coal decomposition frees up space.",
    "",
    "Iron turns into Wrought Iron when heated to 2011K. Wrought Iron tools are better than Iron, and in gt6 it can replace Iron in most machine recipes. But other mods may not recognize Wrought Iron.",
    "",
    "Throw in 15 Coal or Charcoal first, then 35 Magnetite Dust, to smelt 15 portions of Iron.",
  ],
  "quest.52C1F342E19D3574.quest_desc": [
    "GregTech 6's ores generate as Ore Veins or Rock Layers, and Ore Veins generate based on the Rock Layer.",
    "",
    "Rocks matching the Rock Layer generate on the surface.",
    "If there is an Ore Vein below, Rocks containing ore will generate above it.",
    "",
    "Therefore, be sure to waypoint any ore-bearing Rocks you find.",
  ],
  "quest.22B86EDBC7256793.title": "Getting a Metal Anvil",
  "quest.44EB8782F2412B47.title": "More Metal Tools!",
  "quest.6B8F9A91CFD3641F.title": "Advanced Crafting Table",
  "quest.67C022F846587021.quest_desc": [
    "This quest is for players who are really short on Bronze, but I still strongly recommend mining enough Bronze",
  ],
  "quest.3C0D4962070EB7CC.quest_desc": [
    "Vanilla tools' durability has been set extremely low, so don't waste resources on vanilla tools.",
    "",
    "GregTech 6 changes the block-breaking mechanic; now, if the tool isn't suitable, you can't break the block at all.",
    "",
    "GregTech 6 tools' durability consumption is completely different as well; durability is no longer a fixed amount lost per use.",
    "Instead, it's consumed dynamically based on the Mining Level of the broken block and the level of the tool.",
    "",
    "GregTech 6 tools only need a single Stick; sometimes a Bone or Plastic Rod can replace the Stick.",
  ],
  "quest.67C022F846587021.title": "Lead Anvil",
  "quest.1E8B2A8FC92B7ED2.quest_desc": [
    "You can find many Rocks and Sticks on the ground; right-click to pick them up.",
    "",
    "Rocks can be used to make GT stone tools.",
    "",
    "Rocks are also indicators, marking the Rock Layer below.",
    "",
    "If you find Black Granite or Red Granite Rocks, remember to collect plenty.",
    "They make the best stone tools with Mining Level 3.",
  ],
}